{{!--
    The other passengers.

    This is the master section, so its passages can be linked to from any section: the train's
    carriage is the same wherever it is. Each passenger is a passage. Its name is what Zoran sees
    before he looks at them properly ("a man"), with something in brackets where that isn't enough
    to tell two passengers apart. tube.js chooses who's in the carriage from "strangers".

    (again) is whether Zoran has looked at them before, and "noticed" is a list of the people
    who caught his attention, which somebody else has been keeping too.
--}}

[[]]:
{{#unless strangers}}
{{set "strangers" (array
  "a man (Kurt Cobain)"
  "a woman and child"
  "a male student"
  "a fat man"
  "a young pretty woman"
  "a chubby couple"
  "an old woman"
  "a woman (familiar)"
  "a woman (notebook)"
  "two female friends"
  "a man (muttering)"
  "a smiling woman"
  "a man in a suit (earbuds)"
  "a large man"
  "a small woman"
  "a teenage girl"
  "a man (MacBook)"
  "a sleeping man"
  "a woman (magazine)"
  "a man (grey)"
  "a woman drinking coffee"
  "a man in a suit (earring)"
  "a young guy"
  "a man with a moustache"
  "a man in sunglasses"
  "a man (porridge)"
  "a man in a jumper"
  "a scruffy man"
  "a trendy guy"
  "a woman with a baby"
  "a middle-aged woman"
  "a woman reading"
)}}
{{!-- These three are here for the whole journey: they don't get on or off at stations --}}
{{set "settled" (array "a sleeping man" "a woman with a baby" "a woman reading")}}
{{/unless}}

[a man (Kurt Cobain)]:
{{#unless (again)}}
He is reading a biography of Kurt Cobain. The face on the cover is almost life size. If the man holds the book up in the right place, it will be like he lives among us again, albeit with the fixed expression of permanent [self-satisfaction] that the photo on the book's cover gives him.
{{else}}
He is still reading his Kurt Cobain biography.
{{/unless}}

[self-satisfaction]:
{{append "noticed" "the man with the Kurt Cobain biography"}}
Although to be fair, if Kurt Cobain was riding in plain sight, on the tube, having faked his own death and then not noticably aged in any way, he'd have every right to look pleased with himself.

[a woman and child]:
{{#unless (again)}}
Like all children, the boy completely fails to be able to sit still for any length of time. His mother sits next to him, not paying much [attention].
{{else}}
The child has calmed down a bit now, and is looking quietly at his reflection in the window.
{{/unless}}

[attention]:
{{append "noticed" "the woman with the boy who was pulling faces"}}
The child starts pulling faces at the other passengers. What a quandry. This child knows none of the rules. Are we supposed to smile back? Look annoyed? Just ignore him?

[a male student]:
{{#unless (again)}}
He could not look more like a student. He's sat there with a pile of papers, looking through page after page of tables. The stripy jumper, brown trousers and brown shoes finish off the look. He is clearly very comfortable fitting into that category of person. He has fully bought in to that lifestyle and appearance. It makes me feel somewhat jealous actually — I'd never want to look like that, but I'd love to be able to fit in, and know my place, and be happy with it. Maybe in blissful ignorance of other options, but blissful nonetheless.
{{else}}
He's still sitting there, all studenty, looking through his papers.
{{/unless}}

[a fat man]:
{{#unless (again)}}
His belly hangs over his belt. Maybe if he just loosened his belt a bit, his weight wouldn't have been the first thing I noticed.
{{else}}
He is still sitting there, belly calmly flopping onto his lap.
{{/unless}}

[a young pretty woman]:
{{#unless (again)}}
She is incredibly pretty in that fragile sort of way. A fine [bone structure], I suppose, that looks like it might break at any moment.
{{else}}
She remains intact, sat over there, her face all still in one piece. I just hope she never falls over because it looks like her bones couldn't take any kind of impact.
{{/unless}}

[bone structure]:
{{append "noticed" "the woman with the fragile skeleton"}}
Truly an exquisite skeleton. Even her X-rays would have me sighing wistfully. And she knows it.

[a chubby couple]:
{{#unless (again)}}
They are dressed in matching waterproof jackets, and the guy is holding a tatty guide to London. I wonder where they're off to, what their holiday schedule has in store for today.
{{else}}
They are still sat there in their beautifully matching way.
{{/unless}}

[an old woman]:
{{#unless (again)}}
She is sat in the priority seat. At least, I presume it's a she. There is a lot of wispy white hair around the chin area. I would think a man would keep his beard in better shape, and wear it with pride.
{{else}}
She is still that there, quite still — maybe so as not to disturb that beard.
{{/unless}}

[a woman (familiar)]:
{{#unless (again)}}
There's something familiar about her. Where have I seen her before? Was she in the old office? Maybe she was in [something] on telly...
{{else}}
She's still there, and I don't want to risk making eye contact.
{{/unless}}

[something]:
Or maybe it wasn't on telly, maybe it was something I saw [online].

[online]:
{{append "noticed" "the woman who I have seen naked online"}}
Oh God, was she in... I have seen her... well, all of her. Do not catch her eye. Do not catch her eye. I must be bright red. Why is she on the tube anyway? I thought they did all that in America.

[a woman (notebook)]:
{{#unless (again)}}
She is sat scribbling something in a notebook, occasionally looking up and glancing around the carriage, like she's taking [notes] about what she sees.
{{else}}
She's still there, scribbling notes.
{{/unless}}

[notes]:
{{append "noticed" "the woman who was taking surreptitious notes"}}
I can't see what she's writing from this angle. Maybe she's writing a novel. Or practicing to be a spy. Not doing a very good job of it in that case.

[two female friends]:
{{#unless (again)}}
It seems like they don't know each other too well, they're deep in polite [small talk].
{{else}}
They're still there, chatting away.
{{/unless}}

[small talk]:
{{append "noticed" "the two women who were having that inane conversation"}}
It's quite hard to hear above the {{#if leg > 0}}rumble of the tube{{else}}din of the station{{/if}}. What time did that finish... Did you know him from Nottingham... I didn't get back until 2... I don't think I'm missing out on much by not paying attention to this.

[a man (muttering)]:
{{#unless (again)}}
Unlike most people it seems, he's not filling his journey by looking at a newspaper or listening to earphones. He is [muttering] to himself a bit though, as he stares vacantly into space.
{{else}}
He's still muttering occasionally to himself.
{{/unless}}

[muttering]:
{{append "noticed" "the muttering man"}}
Impossible to hear what he's saying. He doesn't seem threatening though, somehow. He seems too nerdy to be dangerous. I mean, he probably has a proper job and everything. Which is probably the perfect cover. He's probably exactly the kind of person that would have somebody kept in his basement.

[a smiling woman]:
{{#unless (again)}}
She is smiling, then suppressing it. But she's not doing very well at suppressing it. I wonder what she's seen. I hope it's not me. My cock's not [hanging out], is it?
{{else}}
She's still sat there, smiling to herself.
{{/unless}}

[hanging out]:
{{append "noticed" "the woman who was smiling as though my cock was hanging out"}}
Of course it's not.

Anyway, if my cock was hanging out, she'd simply faint, right... right? Or pull the alarm lever. Or actually, do absolutely nothing at all. I could [get my cock out] now. What would happen? What would really happen?

[get my cock out]:
I'm not getting my fucking cock out. What is wrong with you?

[a man in a suit (earbuds)]:
{{#unless (again)}}
He listens diligently to his iPod through white earbuds. I can't hear what he's listening to — which is unusual for those stock earbuds. He seems to be listening very carefully though. He has glossy black shoes with bright red laces. The only dash of colour on him. Like a little private joke.
{{else}}
He's still there, tapping his glossy black shoes to the music.
{{/unless}}

[a large man]:
{{#unless (again)}}
He is wearing a leather jacket, a woolly hat, and, bizarrely, shorts. He also has a massive beard — the top half of him must be a lot warmer than the bottom half.
{{else}}
He's still there in his leather jacket, beard and shorts.
{{/unless}}

[a small woman]:
{{#unless (again)}}
She is carrying a massive yellow handbag — almost the same size as she is. I wonder what she keeps in there. She could possibly live in it.
{{else}}
She's still there with her enormous handbag.
{{/unless}}

[a teenage girl]:
{{#unless (again)}}
She is wearing huge bright green headphones on top of her long black hair. She gets her phone out to check her reflection in the camera. That's the second person I've seen doing that this morning. I remember when people used to use mirrors. Using your phone to see what you look like seems like a waste — such an enormous amount of technology for such a very small problem.
{{else}}
She's still there with her massive headphones.
{{/unless}}

[a man (MacBook)]:
{{#unless (again)}}
He is balding, wearing jeans and tatty Converse trainers, and doing something on a Macbook Air. He has a bag stuffed with papers and files.
{{else}}
He's still sat there fiddling with his Macbook.
{{/unless}}

[a sleeping man]:
{{#unless (again)}}
He is a builder by the looks of it — paint-splattered jeans. He is sleeping next to another builder who looks almost the same. These two big burly men look almost cute, blissfully asleep like enormous babies.
{{else}}
Still sound asleep like a baby, along with his mate.
{{/unless}}

[a woman (magazine)]:
{{#unless (again)}}
She is reading some kind of arty magazine.
{{else}}
Still reading that magazine.
{{/unless}}

[a man (grey)]:
{{#unless (again)}}
He is wearing a grey striped shirt, grey trousers and grey shoes. Even his hair is going grey. Mr. Monochrome.
{{else}}
He remains as grey as ever.
{{/unless}}

[a woman drinking coffee]:
{{#unless (again)}}
Her coffee cup has her name scrawled on it in black felt tip. [Roberta]. I know nothing about her, but now I know her name — which is more than I can say for anybody else in this carriage. It's like we have some kind of connection now.
{{else}}
Roberta is still there, proudly displaying her named coffee cup.
{{/unless}}

[Roberta]:
{{append "noticed" "Roberta with her coffee cup"}}
I don't like her name. She looks like she's probably nice, but I think her name is horrible. It's kind of manly. Like Geraldine or Bernardette — big blokey names with a small concession to femininity at the end. Makes me think of an old man with a big beard wearing a flowery dress.

[a man in a suit (earring)]:
{{#unless (again)}}
He has an earring, which I suppose makes him feel more individual than the rest of the people he works with. Yet he still adheres to a dress code. An act of futile rebellion.
{{else}}
He's still sat there with his silly little earring.
{{/unless}}

[a young guy]:
{{#unless (again)}}
He's wearing a T-shirt and picking his nose. Yuck. What can you do though? Ask him to stop? Is he going to [eat that]?
{{else}}
Thankfully he has now stopped picking his nose.
{{/unless}}

[eat that]:
{{append "noticed" "the young guy who was picking his nose"}}
Eugh. What do you want me to do, go up and ask him if I can have some? You're not helping me feel any better.

[a man with a moustache]:
{{#unless (again)}}
Apart from a huge bushy moustache, he is clean shaven. He looks a bit like Lord Kitchener in those posters from the First World War. He might turn round at any second, point at me and say "I want [YOU]".
{{else}}
His moustache is still looking around the carriage, twitchily.
{{/unless}}

[YOU]:
{{append "noticed" "the chap with the Lord Kitchener moustache"}}
It would be nice to feel wanted, but not by somebody who just wants to send me to a pointless death in a muddy hole in France.

[a man in sunglasses]:
{{#unless (again)}}
Sunglasses, down here? And chewing gum, with his mouth wide open. All nonchalant and cool, like he doesn't need to be here, but hey, he just puts up with it, y'know, why complain? So many reasons to hate. He's really playing with that gum as well, like he's totally the boss of it. Everything's going his way — apart from his debilitating eye disorder which means his retinas will fall out if he's exposed to more than two photons at a time. What a colossal prick.
{{else}}
Still no bright sunshine down here.
{{/unless}}

[a man (porridge)]:
{{#unless (again)}}
He's eating a steaming hot tub of porridge. It's pretty hot down here already — surely the last thing anybody needs is piping hot breakfast mush?
{{else}}
He's got rid of his empty tub of porridge — probably left it somewhere he can conveniently forget it.
{{/unless}}

[a man in a jumper]:
{{#unless (again)}}
It's quite a big blue jumper — inappopriately warm for both the weather and the temperature down here on the tube, I should think. And what's that suspicious white stain at the bottom? Does he realise that's there? I hope that's yogurt, or the milk from his breakfast cereal.
{{else}}
Still not sure about that stain.
{{/unless}}

[a scruffy man]:
{{#unless (again)}}
He looks like some sort of academic. Quite poor dress sense — and if I can spot that, it must be really poor dress sense. He's reading some kind of [scientific paper], and chuckling to himself.
{{else}}
He seems to have calmed down about whatever it was that was so funny before in his academic paper.
{{/unless}}

[scientific paper]:
{{append "noticed" "the man who was chuckling at platelet function disorders"}}
Straining to read the title over his shoulder before he turns the page — review... platelet function disorders. I wonder what's so funny about that?

[a trendy guy]:
{{#unless (again)}}
He ticks so many hipster boxes at once. A beard and a flat cap, over which he's wearing huge headphones. Knowingly ironic unfashionable glasses too. I bet they don't even have a prescription. Shorts. Trainers with massive tongues, sticking half way up his shins. Unconventional, yet still a uniform to identify him as a certain type of person, a member of a particular [group].
{{else}}
Look at him sat there with his trendy smug beardy face.
{{/unless}}

[group]:
{{append "noticed" "the hipster with the flat cap and glasses"}}
A group of people who express their identities by trying to look like 1960s Yorkshiremen. Weekdays working down t' mill, weekends working the decks at an eclectic bar in Hoxton? Is that what we're supposed to think? Maybe this guy doesn't know what day of the week it is, so he's hedging his bets.

[a woman with a baby]:
{{#unless (again)}}
The woman is sat in a priority seat with the baby on her lap. The baby is [looking at me].
{{else}}
The baby is looking elsewhere now.
{{/unless}}

[looking at me]:
{{append "noticed" "the woman with the suspicious baby"}}
The baby is staring at me as if he knows something terrible. As if he's accusing me of something. But I haven't done anything. Well, I'm skipping work. But how does the baby know that? He looks like he would tell everybody everything, if only he had the power of language. That's where I win, mate. All you can do is [keep staring].

[keep staring]:
OK, I'd prefer it if he stopped staring to be honest. That's the trouble with babies. No concept of social norms. They don't know that you shouldn't keep staring at people.

[a middle-aged woman]:
{{#unless (again)}}
She reminds me of a teacher I used to have at school. A French teacher, whose name I can't quite remember. Scary lady. Always telling us off for not revising hard enough for her vocab tests. Maybe it is her. Hard to tell. It's been a long time. I'm still slightly scared of her even now, even though she has no power over me, and it might not even be her anyway. Still, best not to risk [attracting her attention].
{{else}}
Too scared to look too much in that direction.
{{/unless}}

[attracting her attention]:
{{append "noticed" "my old French teacher"}}
Why are you making me look? I think it is her. I think that's a mole on her right cheek — it's got to be. I'm not going to say anything. She wouldn't remember me anyway. And besides, I've pretty much forgotten all the French I once knew, and I don't want to get a bollocking. Je voudrais to just stay here quietly, merci beaucoup.

[a woman reading]:
{{#unless (again)}}
She is staring into her e-book reader. I wonder what she's reading. It used to be so much easier when you could see the book cover. Easier to judge. I wonder if it's erotic. Actually, she seems quite bored by whatever it is. Maybe it's some kind of textbook. Her eyelids are slowly drooping.
{{else}}
Pretty sure she's asleep now, unless she can read with her eyes closed.
{{/unless}}

{{!-- These four only get on after Heather has gone (heather.squiffy) --}}

[a man (not Heather)]:
{{#unless (again)}}
He is not Heather. He is unimportant.
{{else}}
He is still not Heather.
{{/unless}}

[a man (pencil legs)]:
{{#unless (again)}}
He has legs like pencils, and impossibly tight [jeans], and a hairstyle.
{{else}}
Still sat there with his pencil legs.
{{/unless}}

[jeans]:
{{append "noticed" "the man with the ridiculously tiny jeans"}}
How does he get into those jeans? Nobody has legs that thin — and those jeans look even thinner than his legs, somehow. It must take him hours to get into those. And the hair. If I put that much effort into my appearance I would just look desparate and ridiculous.

I wonder if he's the type Heather goes for.

I wonder if he is really sad inside.

[a woman (not Heather)]:
{{#unless (again)}}
She is clearly not Heather. Her hair is all wrong. She is irrelevant.
{{else}}
She is still not Heather.
{{/unless}}

[a young woman]:
{{#unless (again)}}
She doesn't look like Heather. Not even like a young Heather. Her face is too round. I have no interest in her.
{{else}}
She still doesn't look like Heather.
{{/unless}}

{{!-- When there's nobody left to look at --}}

[stare]:
{{inc "stares"}}
{{#if stares % 3 = 1}}
I stand for a while staring at the tunnel wall flying by with all its pipes.
{{else if stares = 2}}
I look at the floor, hiding its dirt with its colourful speckles. Clever floor. Also, kind of disgusting.
{{else if stares % 3 = 2}}
I look at the floor, still cleverly hiding its dirt there.
{{else}}
Time continues to elapse.
{{/if}}
